\h Etana
\toc1 Etana
\mt1 Etana
\c 1
\p
\v 1 \vp 1\vp* From the young of the eagle [the eagle went forth?].
\q1 His heart prompted the eagle [and * * *]
\q1 He considered and his heart prompted him
\q1 to eat the young of his companion [the serpent?].
\q1 The eagle opened his mouth, and spoke to his young:
\q1 “I will eat the young of the serpent, the serpent, whose heart is [without cares?].
\q1 I will ascend and spy from the height of the heaven,
\q1 I will swoop down upon the top of the tree and I will eat (the serpent’s?) brood.”
\q1 One of the young birds, endowed with much wisdom, said to his father, the eagle:
\q1 “Do not eat, O my father! Shall the net of Shamash (the Sun-god) catch thee?
\q1 Shall the snare and the ban of Shamash fall upon thee and catch thee?
\q1 Whoso transgresses the law of Shamash, from him Shamash [will exact revenge!].”
\q1 But he did not hearken to them, and gave no heed to the word of his young one.
\q1 He swooped down and ate the young of the serpent.
\q1 This [was done?] in the middle of the day, but the serpent [was away?].
\q1 * * * carrying his burden [he came to his nest in the evening].
\q1 [But found] its nest [empty and its young destroyed?].
\v 2 \vp 2\vp* When the serpent had come, he said unto Shamash:
\q1 “I will tell thee [what has happened to me?]
\q1 To the eagle * * *
\q1 Then he spied * * * my nest,
\q1 my nest he spied, that was in the tree.
\q1 Now my young are killed, for I was not there,
\q1 when he swooped down and ate them up.
\q1 Let [him now pay for?] the evil which he has done, O Shamash!
\q1 For, Shamash, thy net is the broad earth
\q1 and thy snare the distant heaven.
\q1 Who has ever escaped from thy net?
\q1 Even Zu, the worker of evil, who carries high his evil head, [did not escape]!”
\q1 When he had thus listened to the prayer of the serpent,
\q1 Shamash opened his mouth, and said to the serpent:
\q1 “Go now, and take the road to the mountain,
\q1 I will get (?) for thee a [dead] wild ox.
\q1 Open its bowels, and crawl into its belly,
\q1 and take up thy dwelling in its belly.
\q1 All kinds of birds of heaven shall swoop down
\q1 and [will eat of the flesh?];
\q1 and the eagle shall come with them;
\q1 and not knowing [thy intention and plot],
\q1 he will seek to get a piece of the flesh, moving swiftly to and fro,
\q1 and will make for the hidden parts.
\q1 And when he has penetrated into the inner parts, seize him by his wing,
\q1 tear off his wing, his feather, his pinion;
\q1 tear him to pieces and cast him into a pit,
\q1 that he may die a death from hunger and thirst.”
\q1 At the word of Shamash, the hero, the serpent departed and went into the mountain.
\q1 And the serpent found the [dead] wild ox,
\q1 opened its bowels and penetrated into its belly,
\q1 and took up his dwelling in its belly.
\q1 All kinds of birds of heaven swooped down and ate of the flesh.
\q1 “But is the eagle to suspect the evil intent and
\q1 will he not eat of the meat with the young birds?”
\q1 The eagle opened his mouth and said to his young:
\q1 “Come, let us swoop down and eat of the flesh of this wild ox!”
\q1 And one of the young birds, endowed with much wisdom, said to his father, the eagle:
\q1 “Do not swoop down, my father! Suppose there lurks the serpent in [the flesh of] this wild ox!”
\q1 But the eagle [opened his mouth] and said these words:
\q1 “* * * * * * * * * *”
\q1 He did not hearken to them, and gave no heed to the word of his young one.
\q1 He swooped down and stood upon the wild ox.
\q1 The eagle examined the carcass, looking carefully before and behind him.
\q1 He again examined the carcass, looking carefully before and behind him.
\q1 Then, moving swiftly, he made for what was hidden in its interior.
\q1 And when he had penetrated into its interior, the serpent seized him by his wing.
\q1 * * * * * * *
\q1 The eagle opened his mouth, and spoke to the serpent:
\q1 “Have mercy upon me and I will present thee with a gift, such as a bridegroom gives.”
\q1 The serpent opened his mouth, and spoke to the eagle:
\q1 “If I release thee, Shamash will be against us both, as [formerly against thee?].
\q1 His wrath against thee will then be turned upon me,
\q1 which now, as a punishment, I execute on thee.”
\q1 So he tore off his wing, his feather, his pinion;
\q1 he tore him to pieces and cast him into a pit,
\q1 that he should die a death from hunger and thirst.
\v 3 \vp 3\vp* [But the eagle did not die; for Etana, whose wife could not give birth to a son, said thus]:
\v 4 \vp 4\vp* “The seer-priests have completed my offerings,
\q1 They have finished my sacrifices, the joy of the heart of the gods.
\q1 My lord, let now proceed from thy mouth the revelation,
\q1 and give [me?] the ‘Plant of Birth’!
\q1 Shew me the ‘Plant of Birth’!
\q1 Tear out my offspring [from his mother’s womb?] and grant me a son.”
\q1 And Shamash opened his mouth and said to Etana:
\q1 “Go, and take the road across the mountain.”
\v 5 \vp 5\vp* [Here he was to find the eagle, who would give him the desired Plant of Birth.]
\v 6 \vp 6\vp* And the eagle opened his mouth and spoke to Shamash, his lord:
\q1 “* * * * * * * * * *
\q1 The young of a bird [he shall bring unto me?]
\q1 * * * * * * * * * *
\q1 Whatsoever he will say unto me, I will hear;
\q1 and whatsoever I shall say unto him, may he do!”
\q1 And Etana did as he was told by Shamash, the hero.
\q1 He took the young of a bird and went to the eagle.
\q1 The eagle opened his mouth and spoke to Etana:
\q1 “What is the desire that has brought thee hither?”
\q1 And Etana opened his mouth (and) said to the eagle:
\q1 “My friend, give me the ‘Plant of Birth’;
\q1 shew me the ‘Plant of Birth’!
\q1 Tear out my offspring [from his mother’s womb?] and grant me a son.
\q1 And in order to get the ‘Plant of Birth,’
\q1 so that he, who is now [inclosed in the womb?] may come forth,
\q1 Shamash, my lord, said unto me:
\q1 ‘Go, and take the road across the mountain,
\q1 [there thou shalt get?] the “Plant of Birth.”’”
\v 7 \vp 7\vp* On one occasion the eagle carried Etana up to heaven. The hero clung to the eagle’s wings and they rose together until they could see the gates of heaven. There:
\v 8 \vp 8\vp* “At the entrance to the gate of Anu, Bel, and Ea we fell down worshipping.
\q1 At the entrance to the gate of Sin, Shamash, Adad, and [Ishtar?] we fell down worshipping.
\q1 [Four lines wanting.]
\q1 A throne was there erected and [* * *]
\q1 Beneath the throne lions were lying down.
\q1 I drew nigh, and the lions jumped up.
\q1 I became frightened and trembled * * *”
\q1 But the eagle said to Etana:
\q1 “My friend, let thy countenance brighten up.
\q1 Come, let me carry thee up to the heaven of Anu.
\q1 Against my breast lay [thy back?],
\q1 upon the wing-feathers (?) of my pinions lay [thy hands],
\q1 upon my side lay [thy side?]!”
\q1 Against his breast he laid [his back?].
\q1 Upon the wing-feathers (?) of his pinions he laid his hands.
\q1 Upon his side he laid his side.
\q1 He made (fast) his hold and great was the weight. After the eagle had carried
\q1 Etana for two hours, he said to him:
\q1 “Behold, my friend, the land, how it is!
\q1 Look at the sea at the sides of E-kur!
\q1 The land looks [as if it were] only a mountain; the sea has become
\q1 like a [small pool of] water.”
\q1 After the eagle had carried Etana for four hours,
\q1 he said to him:
\q1 “Behold, my friend, the land, how it is! the land * * *”
\q1 After the eagle had carried Etana for six hours, he said to him:
\q1 “Behold, my friend, the land, how it is!
\q1 The sea has become like the [water-ditch] of a gardener.”
\q1 After going thus up to the heaven of Anu,
\q1 the eagle and Etana came through the gate of
\q1 Anu, Bel, and Ea, and there fell down worshipping
\q1 * * * the eagle and Etana * * *
\v 9 \vp 9\vp* [Another quite mutilated column contains a repetition of the flight of Etana and the eagle to the heaven of Anu.]
\v 10 \vp 10\vp* After they had travelled for six hours, Etana said to the eagle:
\v 11 \vp 11\vp* “My friend, I will not rise up to heaven; hold on, that I [may return to earth?].”
\v 12 \vp 12\vp* Etana “falls down one double hour,” and the eagle flies down with him; a second and a third double hour they go downward together; both fall down together upon the ground.
\v 13 \vp 13\vp* The eagle bursts asunder; of Etana [nothing is left?].
